# Conventional commits

This repo uses **[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)**. After every completed change (feature, fix, docs, chore, etc.), **suggest** a commit message in that format. Do **not** create the git commit unless the user explicitly asks.

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

- **type** (required): `feat` | `fix` | `docs` | `style` | `refactor` | `perf` | `test` | `chore` | `ci` | `build` | `revert`
- **scope** (optional): short area — component/id (`tooltip`, `file`), surface (`demo`, `manifest`), or comma-separated when one commit spans peers (`mesh, toolpath`)
- **description**: imperative, lowercase start, no trailing period; focus on **why** / outcome, not a file list
- **body** (optional): markdown-friendly detail; when suggesting in chat, a short bullet list is fine for the body
- **breaking**: `feat(scope)!: …` or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer

## Examples (match existing history)

```
feat: implement required fields
feat(tooltip): configurable width and wrap
fix(file): dropzone file gap
fix(mesh, toolpath): home view interrupts animation
style(page-nav): add hover/click styles
chore(manifest): regen
docs(usage): document required fields
```

## Agent behaviour

1. Finish the change, then propose **one** subject line (and body bullets only if useful).
2. Prefer a single coherent commit per logical change; split only when the user asks or the work clearly spans unrelated concerns.
3. Use `chore(manifest): regen` (or similar) when the only follow-up is regenerating `framework-manifest.json`.
4. Never invent a non-conventional subject style (`Updated X`, `WIP`, etc.) in suggestions.
5. For multi-step plans, suggest a commit after **each** step and wait for `continue` — see [plan-execution](plan-execution.mdc).
